   "answer": "The largest power bank you can generally bring on a plane is 160 Wh (watt-hours), but anything over 100 Wh requires airline approval under U.S. FAA rules.\n\nPower bank limits for flights\n\nCapacity\n\n\t\n\nAllowed?\n\n\t\n\nAirline approval\n\n\n\n\nUp to 100 Wh\n\n\t\n\n Yes\n\n\t\n\nNot required\n\n\n\n\nOver 100–160 Wh\n\n\t\n\n Usually\n\n\t\n\nRequired\n\n\n\n\nOver 160 Wh\n\n\t\n\n No\n\n\t\n\nNot permitted\n\nImportant: Power banks must go in your carry-on baggage, never in checked luggage.\n\nWhat does that mean in mAh?\n\nAssuming a typical lithium battery rated at 3.7 V:\n\nCapacity\n\n\t\n\nEnergy\n\n\t\n\nStatus\n\n\n\n\n20,000 mAh\n\n\t\n\n74 Wh\n\n\t\n\nAllowed\n\n\n\n\n26,800 mAh\n\n\t\n\n99.2 Wh\n\n\t\n\nAllowed\n\n\n\n\n27,000 mAh\n\n\t\n\n99.9 Wh\n\n\t\n\nAllowed\n\n\n\n\n30,000 mAh\n\n\t\n\n111 Wh\n\n\t\n\nApproval required\n\n\n\n\n40,000 mAh\n\n\t\n\n148 Wh\n\n\t\n\nApproval required\n\n\n\n\n43,200 mAh\n\n\t\n\n159.8 Wh\n\n\t\n\nApproval required\n\n\n\n\n50,000 mAh\n\n\t\n\n185 Wh\n\n\t\n\nNot allowed\n\nBest choice: A power bank rated at or below 100 Wh (typically around 27,000 mAh) avoids the airline approval requirement.\n\nOne important exception: 2026 IATA guidance limits power banks to 100 Wh, and some airlines, including American Airlines, now enforce that limit. \nIATA\n+1\n\nWhich airline are you flying, and is it a domestic U.S. or international flight? I can tell you the maximum allowed for your specific trip."
